So a naive token scan will have false positives in prose. I need a smarter scan: strip comments before scanning. Writing a proper Lean comment stripper is a bit of work but doable with a simple state machine handling `--` line comments, `/- ... -/` block comments (nestable), and string literals.

Let me implement a small stripper in the audit script. That's the robust approach.

Let me write it:
```python
def strip_lean_comments(text: str) -> str:
    out = []
    i = 0
    n = len(text)
    depth = 0
    in_line_comment = False
    in_string = False
    while i < n:
        c = text[i]
        if in_line_comment:
            if c == '\n':
                in_line_comment = False
                out.append(c)
            else:
                out.append(' ')
            i += 1
            continue
        if in_string:
            out.append(c)
            if c == '\\':
                out.append(text[i+1] if i+1 < n else '')
                i += 2
                continue
            if c == '"':
                in_string = False
            i += 1
            continue
        if depth > 0:
            if text.startswith('/-', i):
                depth += 1; out.append('  '); i += 2; continue
            if text.startswith('-/', i):
                depth -= 1; out.append('  '); i += 2; continue
            out.append('\n' if c == '\n' else ' ')
            i += 1
            continue
        # normal
        if text.startswith('--', i):
            in_line_comment = True; out.append('  '); i += 2; continue
        if text.startswith('/-', i):
            depth = 1; out.append('  '); i += 2; continue
        if c == '"':
            in_string = True; out.append(c); i += 1; continue
        out.append(c); i += 1
    return ''.join(out)
```
